                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, thank you for referring this 67-year-old female patient who presented with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. Clinical examination revealed marked tenderness at McBurney's point. Laboratory workup demonstrated leukocytosis, and abdominal ultrasonography confirmed acute appendicitis without evidence of perforation. The patient was admitted and prepared for surgical intervention. A laparoscopic appendectomy was performed without intraoperative complications. Postoperatively, the patient received intravenous fluid replacement, analgesic medication, and antibiotic therapy. Wound care was provided regularly, and vital signs remained stable throughout the hospital course. The patient mobilized well and tolerated oral intake without difficulty. She is discharged in good general condition with outpatient wound follow-up recommended. Please continue monitoring for any signs of surgical site infection. Discharge medications and further instructions have been provided to the patient.</div>"
            },
